  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Rounds of showers and thunderstorms with locally heavy rain,\n   the potential for flash flooding, and gusty winds (mainly\n   western Montana) through early this evening.\n\n-  Warming, overall drying through the weekend with afternoon\n   chances for showers and thunderstorms.\n\nHeavy rainfall and isolated flash flooding remain the primary \noperational concerns today. Ongoing morning showers and embedded\nthunderstorms across southwest and west-central Montana have\nalready produced rain rates between 0.25 and 0.50 inches per hour.\nAs atmospheric instability builds through this afternoon, slow-\nmoving or training thunderstorms will redevelop across north-\ncentral Idaho and primarily western Montana. With above-normal\nprecipitable water values and training storm potential, localized\nflash flooding and subsequent debris flow activity remains a\ndistinct hazard especially in and downstream of burn scars, steep\nterrain, and poor-drainage urban locations. Activity will peak\nthrough late afternoon before diminishing around 7:00 PM MDT.\nDownburst outflow winds reaching up to 40-45 mph and small hail\nwill accompany the stronger cells. \n\nAn upper-level ridge begins rebuilding over the western US this\nweekend, initiating a gradual warming and overall drying trend.\nHowever, persistent southwesterly flow aloft will continually\ntransport monsoonal moisture out of the Great Basin and into the \nregion, maintaining daily chances for scattered afternoon showers\nand thunderstorms through early next week. \n\nEnsemble guidance has indicated a ridge-flatting trough passage\nout of Canada in the Tuesday to Wednesday time frame, which could\npromote more widespread shower and thunderstorm chances\nindependent of the diurnal thunderstorm regime. This weather\nfeature will also likely promote an increase in surface westerly \nwind speeds.\n\nRegional wildfire smoke will begin returning aloft in the coming \ndays, but surface smoke concentrations are expected to remain \nlight at this time.\n\nHigh amounts of low-level moisture from today's rainfall combined\nwith overnight decoupling and clearing skies will favor fog and\nlow stratus formation in mountain valleys through the overnight\nperiod into Saturday morning. Good to excellent overnight relative\nhumidities are expected helping to curb fire activity overnight\nand shortening burn windows. Daily thunderstorm activity will\nintroduce concerns for new lightning starts over the coming days.",
